WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.635
Welcome to another bonus episode of How We Made Your Mother.
欢迎收看《我们如何创造了你的母亲》的另一集奖励。

00:00:03.635 --> 00:00:13.511
We're hard at work on season two, which we'll launch very, very soon, and we're using this space now to share some appearances of the team on other great podcasts.
我们正在努力制作第二季，我们很快就会推出，我们现在利用这个空间来分享团队在其他精彩播客上的一些表现。

00:00:13.511 --> 00:00:17.267
And today we bring you Craig Thomas' appearance on Hyperfocus.
今天我们为您带来克雷格·托马斯 (Craig Thomas) 在《Hyperfocus》节目中的亮相。

00:00:17.267 --> 00:00:23.144
Hyperfocus is a show that zeros in on what fascinates us about ADHD, mental health, and learning.
《Hyperfocus》是一档聚焦多动症、心理健康和学习方面让我们着迷的节目。

00:00:23.144 --> 00:00:32.959
On this episode, Craig will talk about his son Elliot, who was born with Jacobson's syndrome, a genetic condition causing physical and developmental disabilities.
在本集中，克雷格将谈论他的儿子埃利奥特，他出生时患有雅各布森综合症，这是一种导致身体和发育障碍的遗传性疾病。

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:43.320
When Elliot was born, Craig's career was taking off with How I Met Your Mother, and he says, I was living an hour drama show at home and then driving to work and doing a sitcom.
艾略特出生时，克雷格的事业随着《老爸老妈的浪漫史》而起飞，他说，我在家看一个小时的戏剧，然后开车去上班，拍一部情景喜剧。

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:49.520
And his upcoming novel, That's Not How It Happened, is based in part on his family's experience.
他即将出版的小说《事情不是这样发生的》部分是基于他家人的经历。

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:54.359
Check out Craig now on Hyper Focus.
现在就来看看克雷格的《Hyper Focus》吧。

00:00:54.359 --> 00:01:02.600
It's strange how we as a society don't have more and better answers for what adult lives look like for people with disabilities.
奇怪的是，作为一个社会，我们对于残疾人的成年生活是什么样子却没有更多更好的答案。

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:04.280
People with disabilities are human.
残疾人也是人。

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:10.670
They have parents and families who are humans and love them and deserve respect and decency and humanity dignity.
他们的父母和家庭都是人类，爱他们，值得尊重、体面和人性尊严。

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:12.700
That's Craig Thomas.
那是克雷格·托马斯。

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:15.859
You might not know his name, but you definitely know his work.
你可能不知道他的名字，但你一定知道他的作品。

00:01:15.859 --> 00:01:26.180
He co-created How I Met Your Mother, and he's also been a writer on American Dad, the late show with David Letterman, made music for Sesame Street, and even written for The New Yorker.
他是《老爸老妈的浪漫史》的共同创作者，也是《美国爸爸》、大卫·莱特曼深夜秀的编剧，为《芝麻街》创作音乐，甚至为《纽约客》撰稿。

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:33.859
There are really colorful, wonderful ways to describe something that is absurd, stupid, ridiculous, hypocritical, hapless.
确实有很多丰富多彩、美妙的方式来描述荒谬、愚蠢、可笑、虚伪、不幸的事情。

00:01:33.859 --> 00:01:45.459
There are so many funny ways to talk about those things without resorting to this incredibly easy punching-down, useless, outdated way of calling something those things.
有很多有趣的方式来谈论这些事情，而不必诉诸这种极其简单的、无用的、过时的方式来称呼这些东西。

00:01:45.459 --> 00:01:51.360
If you've been listening to the last two episodes of Hyper Focus, you've already heard from Craig.
如果您已经听过《Hyper Focus》的最后两集，那么您已经听过克雷格的来信。

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:57.803
As we looked at how language, and especially the R word, echoes through comedy, culture, and everyday life.
当我们观察语言，尤其是 R 词如何在喜剧、文化和日常生活中产生共鸣时。

00:01:57.803 --> 00:02:00.934
But this episode is about a lot more than that word.
但这一集的意义远不止这个词。

00:02:00.934 --> 00:02:05.029
It's about Craig's journey as a father of a child with disabilities.
这是关于克雷格作为一名残疾儿童的父亲的旅程。

00:02:05.029 --> 00:02:16.710
Because 18 years ago, while Craig was running a hit TV show, his son Elliot was born with a rare genetic condition called Jacobson Syndrome, which causes physical and developmental disabilities.
因为 18 年前，当克雷格正在主持一档热门电视节目时，他的儿子艾略特出生时患有一种罕见的遗传性疾病，称为雅各布森综合症，会导致身体和发育障碍。

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:19.419
And his life and perspective changed forever.
他的生活和观点永远改变了。

00:02:19.419 --> 00:02:28.180
It'll be no surprise to anybody that Craig is very funny, but he's also a really thoughtful person when it comes to parenting, disability, language, and media.
克雷格非常有趣，这对任何人来说都不会感到惊讶，但在育儿、残疾、语言和媒体方面，他也是一个非常有思想的人。

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:36.979
And he has a unique perspective on what it feels like to look back at the culture that you helped shape and make the choice to do things differently.
他对回顾你帮助塑造的文化并做出以不同方式做事的选择的感觉有独特的视角。

00:02:36.979 --> 00:02:43.800
And another reason we wanted to talk to Craig is because he recently tried his hand at something new, a novel.
我们想与克雷格交谈的另一个原因是他最近尝试了一些新的东西，一本小说。

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:46.039
It's called That's Not How It Happened.
这就是所谓的“事情不是这样发生的”。

00:02:46.039 --> 00:02:54.080
And it's inspired by his family's story, a story that, like this episode, is as much about music and humor as it is about fear and love.
它的灵感来自他家人的故事，这个故事就像这一集一样，既关于音乐和幽默，也关于恐惧和爱。

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:57.919
He's not reducible to WebMD page of symptoms.
他无法将症状简化为 WebMD 页面。

00:02:57.919 --> 00:02:59.560
He is not reducible to the R word.
他不能简化为 R 词。

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:04.900
He is not reducible to anything because he is a beautiful human being.
他不能被简化为任何东西，因为他是一个美丽的人。

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:16.419
I'm Ray Jacobson, and today on Hyper Focus, a longer conversation with Craig Thomas.
我是雷·雅各布森，今天在 Hyper Focus 节目中与克雷格·托马斯进行了较长的对话。

00:03:16.419 --> 00:03:19.020
So how much your mother ended 10 years ago?
那么你妈妈10年前的结局是多少呢？

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:19.939
I lived in Los Angeles.
我住在洛杉矶。

00:03:19.939 --> 00:03:21.740
I'm from New York, originally.
我原来是纽约人。

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:32.580
And we, my wife and I and my writing partner Carter, we all went to college together and we moved out to LA and we lived there for like 12 years, about 10 of which were doing homage to mother.
我们，我和我的妻子以及我的写作搭档卡特，我们一起上了大学，然后搬到了洛杉矶，我们在那里住了大约 12 年，其中大约 10 年是在向母亲致敬。

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:45.099
But we moved back here when the show ended and part of what happened during the run of the show, we had our son in between your two and three of how much mother and we had no idea what was gonna happen.
但是当节目结束时我们搬回了这里，并且在节目运行期间发生了部分事情，我们让我们的儿子在你们两三个人之间，有多少母亲和我们不知道会发生什么。

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:50.719
We had no idea in utero that he would be born with what turned out to be a very rare genetic syndrome.
我们在子宫内并不知道他出生时就会患有一种非常罕见的遗传综合症。

00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:54.659
with all of these challenges, we'll get into it.
面对所有这些挑战，我们将全力以赴。

00:03:54.659 --> 00:03:58.460
Health challenges, you needed open heart surgery at two weeks old.
健康挑战，您在两周大时需要进行心脏直视手术。

00:03:58.460 --> 00:04:03.699
And then we only found out after the open heart surgery that it was all part of this larger syndrome called Jacobson syndrome.
然后我们在心脏直视手术后才发现，这都是雅各布森综合症这一更大综合症的一部分。

00:04:03.699 --> 00:04:06.139
Not spelled exactly like your last name, right?
拼写与你的姓氏不完全一样，对吗？

00:04:06.139 --> 00:04:07.300
The Swedish way, right?
瑞典方式，对吧？

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:08.139
It's a Swedish way.
这是瑞典人的方式。

00:04:08.139 --> 00:04:10.500
Yes, it is discovered by a Swedish doctor, so well played.
是的，是瑞典医生发现的，打得很好。

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:16.579
Yes, that's you discovered it named after Jacobson and Sweden, that Dr. Jacobson, but we say Jacobson here.
是的，你发现它以雅各布森和瑞典命名，即雅各布森博士，但我们在这里说雅各布森。

00:04:17.939 --> 00:04:21.729
And it's, you know, it's this really complicated, rare syndrome.
你知道，这是一种非常复杂、罕见的综合症。

00:04:21.729 --> 00:04:24.985
It's a deletion of part of one of the 11th chromosomes.
这是第 11 号染色体之一的部分缺失。

00:04:24.985 --> 00:04:30.905
And it has medical ramifications and developmental, intellectual ramifications, all kinds of things.
它具有医学影响、发展、智力影响以及各种各样的影响。

00:04:30.905 --> 00:04:38.780
And we, you know, and yet I still had to be running this comedy TV show every week, you know, it was this really interesting balance.
我们，你知道，但我仍然必须每周运行这个喜剧电视节目，你知道，这是一种非常有趣的平衡。

00:04:38.779 --> 00:04:46.008
But you know, he said, I was sort of living, I was living an hour drama show at home and then driving to work and doing a sitcom.
但你知道，他说，我有点生活，我在家里看一个小时的戏剧，然后开车去上班，拍一部情景喜剧。

00:04:46.008 --> 00:04:47.745
It was just the weirdest thing.
这是最奇怪的事情。

00:04:47.745 --> 00:04:52.060
I was like a full, a whole slate of content, traumatic and community content.
我就像是一整套内容、创伤性内容和社区内容。

00:04:53.819 --> 00:04:55.147
And so, yeah, we moved.
所以，是的，我们搬家了。

00:04:55.147 --> 00:05:00.285
My wife and I really wanted to move back where we had family support, where we were from.
我和我的妻子真的很想搬回我们有家人支持的地方，我们来自的地方。

00:05:00.285 --> 00:05:03.632
So that's what we did at the end of How I Met Your Mother.
这就是我们在《老爸老妈的浪漫史》结尾时所做的。

00:05:03.632 --> 00:05:08.135
And professionally, since then, I've, well, for one thing, we had another kid.
从职业角度来说，从那时起，我，嗯，一方面，我们有了另一个孩子。

00:05:08.135 --> 00:05:09.982
And so I have my daughter Celia.
所以我有了我的女儿西莉亚。

00:05:09.982 --> 00:05:10.617
She's nine.
她九岁了。

00:05:10.617 --> 00:05:11.945
And she's neurotypical.
而且她很神经质。

00:05:11.945 --> 00:05:14.254
She's a healthy, precocious little girl.
她是一个健康、早熟的小女孩。

00:05:14.254 --> 00:05:20.084
She's very, she's different from her brother and yet they connect quite beautifully, the two of them.
她和她哥哥非常不同，但他们两个的联系却非常美妙。

00:05:20.084 --> 00:05:25.336
Despite this nine-year age gap, my son just turned 18, which is a whole other conversation.
尽管年龄相差九岁，但我儿子刚刚满 18 岁，这完全是另一回事了。

00:05:25.336 --> 00:05:26.779
I have so many questions.
我有很多问题。

00:05:26.779 --> 00:05:29.180
Seven-year-old, yeah.
七岁了，是的。

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:31.579
And so my daughter's nine, my son is 18.
我女儿 9 岁，我儿子 18 岁。

00:05:31.579 --> 00:05:47.419
And we move back here and part of my mission, after moving back, well, one of my missions was to not be such a workaholic and be more available to say, have a second child and be with my children because running a TV show is such a full-time job, so all-consuming.
我们搬回这里，也是我使命的一部分，搬回来之后，嗯，我的使命之一就是不要成为一个工作狂，更愿意说，生第二个孩子，和我的孩子们在一起，因为经营电视节目是一项全职工作，非常耗时。

00:05:47.419 --> 00:05:55.519
And then, you know, like I said, we didn't know we were about to become the parents of a child with a disability, and suddenly you are.
然后，你知道，就像我说的，我们不知道我们即将成为一个残疾孩子的父母，而突然间你就知道了。

00:05:55.519 --> 00:06:00.620
And there were six more years of how much a mother to do, or however many more years.
作为一个母亲，还有六年的时间，或者还有多少年的时间。

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:02.420
And it was like, it was a lot.
就好像，很多。

00:06:02.420 --> 00:06:03.800
It was a lot to juggle.
有很多事情需要兼顾。

00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:24.139
So since then, I have had part of my mission, besides finding more balance, has been finding ways to write about this, finding ways to write about this experience, of becoming the parent, my son, Elliot, and all of the challenges and fears that came with that and all of the massive, beautiful gifts that have come from being Elliot's dad.
所以从那时起，我的使命的一部分，除了找到更多的平衡之外，一直在寻找方法来写这个，想办法写这个经历，成为父母，我的儿子艾略特，以及随之而来的所有挑战和恐惧，以及作为艾略特父亲所带来的所有巨大而美丽的礼物。

00:06:24.139 --> 00:06:33.259
And so I've been trying, I tried for a couple years, a few years in TV and film to do projects that incorporated aspects of that part of my life.
所以我一直在尝试，我尝试了几年，在电视和电影领域尝试了几年，去做一些融合了我生活的这一部分的项目。

00:06:33.259 --> 00:06:40.100
And it's really hard to get Hollywood to green light TV shows or movies about those topics sometimes.
有时，要让好莱坞批准有关这些主题的电视节目或电影确实很难。

00:06:40.100 --> 00:06:41.925
very close on a couple things.
在一些事情上非常接近。

00:06:41.925 --> 00:06:49.716
There's still one that I'm still trying to push up the hill a little bit, but this is a very long answer to what am I doing now.
还有一个我仍在尝试将其推上山，但这是对我现在正在做的事情的一个很长的答案。

00:06:49.716 --> 00:06:50.933
What am I doing now?
我现在在做什么？

00:06:50.933 --> 00:06:56.350
The last two or three years, I've written my first novel, and it is very much about this.
在过去的两三年里，我写了我的第一部小说，它与此有关。

00:06:56.350 --> 00:07:04.201
The book is called That's Not How It Happened, and it is, I hope, a funny, and yet going into luck at a family kind of like mine.
这本书的名字是《事情不是这样发生的》，我希望这本书很有趣，但在像我这样的家庭中却很幸运。

00:07:04.201 --> 00:07:07.001
It's not exactly my family, but it's a family.
这不完全是我的家人，但它是一个家庭。

00:07:07.001 --> 00:07:08.949
It's a mom, a dad, and two kids.
这是一个妈妈、一个爸爸和两个孩子。

00:07:08.949 --> 00:07:14.913
There's a son who's about 24 with Down Syndrome, and he's sort of entering into adult life, right?
有一个大约 24 岁的儿子，患有唐氏综合症，他已经进入成人生活了，对吧？

00:07:14.913 --> 00:07:20.147
He's in that process of what does adult life look like for somebody with a disability.
他正处于残疾人成年生活的过程中。

00:07:20.147 --> 00:07:30.738
And he has a younger sister who in the novel is 17, coming up and graduating high school, and the mother and the father in their various ways are sort of standing on a cliff.
他还有一个妹妹，在小说中她已经 17 岁了，即将高中毕业，而母亲和父亲以各种方式都有点站在悬崖上。

00:07:30.738 --> 00:07:40.110
Whenever I talk to somebody about, you know, another parent with a disability that has a child with a disability who is coming up on 18, 19, 20 years old.
每当我和某人谈论另一位残疾父母，他们有一个即将 18、19、20 岁的残疾孩子。

00:07:40.110 --> 00:07:42.971
Without fail, they use the metaphor of a cliff.
他们无一例外地使用了悬崖的比喻。

00:07:42.971 --> 00:07:44.980
That's what it feels like, right?
就是这样的感觉，对吧？

00:07:44.980 --> 00:07:47.256
They go, well, we're coming up on that cliff.
他们走了，好吧，我们快到悬崖了。

00:07:47.256 --> 00:07:53.377
And I would say my novel is sort of about that cliff and about sort of what coming up on that cliff does for all of them.
我想说我的小说是关于那个悬崖的，以及关于登上那个悬崖对他们所有人的影响的。

00:07:53.377 --> 00:08:01.420
And the little cliffs they're all kind of standing on of like these inflection points that will lead in different directions for all four lives in this family.
他们所站立的小悬崖就像这些拐点，将引导这个家庭的四个人走向不同的方向。

00:08:01.420 --> 00:08:06.276
So the book is told from four perspectives, all four of the narrators are those four characters.
所以这本书是从四个角度讲述的，四个叙述者都是这四个人物。

00:08:06.276 --> 00:08:07.945
It's such a huge part of my life.
这是我生活中很重要的一部分。

00:08:07.945 --> 00:08:10.677
Writers write what they know a great deal of the time.
作家大多时候写他们所知道的东西。

00:08:10.677 --> 00:08:15.381
And I thought, I need to write something and put something out in the world about this topic.
我想，我需要写一些关于这个话题的东西并向世界发布一些东西。

00:08:15.381 --> 00:08:26.662
And the best way to do it was for the final product to be words on a page rather than a script that a studio needs to green light and spend millions of dollars on, which just wasn't necessarily happening when I wrote those.
最好的方法是让最终产品成为页面上的文字，而不是工作室需要批准并花费数百万美元的脚本，而当我编写这些脚本时，这不一定会发生。

00:08:26.662 --> 00:08:28.179
So that is a very long answer.
所以这是一个很长的答案。

00:08:28.180 --> 00:08:30.252
Forgive me of what am I doing now?
请原谅我现在在做什么？

00:08:30.252 --> 00:08:33.299
That was a 10 year ramp up to what am I doing now?
那是我现在所做的事情的十年进步？

00:08:33.299 --> 00:08:52.460
That's amazing, and first of all, I love a thorough answer, and suddenly, you know, the idea of having an 18-year-old kid, period, let alone an 18-year-old kid who has disabilities who is going into the world in this new way, like the Cliff metaphor, I can feel it, this release.
这太棒了，首先，我喜欢一个彻底的答案，突然间，你知道，有一个 18 岁孩子的想法，就这样，更不用说一个 18 岁的残疾孩子，他将以这种新的方式进入世界，就像悬崖的隐喻一样，我能感觉到，这个版本。

00:08:52.460 --> 00:08:53.460
Yes, yes.
是的，是的。

00:08:53.460 --> 00:08:56.419
It's always the metaphor, and I wrote it into the book.
这总是比喻，我把它写进了书里。

00:08:56.419 --> 00:09:11.728
I wrote it into the book that way, because everyone says it that way, because it feels that way, it's such an unknown and it's strange how we as a society don't have more and better answers for what adult lives look like for people with disabilities, right?
我就是这样写进书里的，因为每个人都这么说，因为感觉都是这样，这是一个未知数，奇怪的是我们作为一个社会对于残疾人的成人生活是什么样子没有更多更好的答案，对吧？

00:09:11.728 --> 00:09:23.940
I mean, it's hard enough sort of navigating everything, educationally, everything, all of your child's health needs, health insurance, all those things that are part of child, that IEPs, all of that stuff.
我的意思是，在教育方面，所有事情，所有孩子的健康需求，健康保险，所有属于孩子一部分的事情，IEP，所有这些东西，都已经够难了。

00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:35.460
But then there is some sort of a roadmap and then sort of right around 21 and getting into that you really, you start to feel like you are looking down on this cliff, and you go, I don't know what's down there.
但是然后有某种路线图，然后大约在 21 点左右，进入那个你真的，你开始感觉你正在俯视这个悬崖，然后你走，我不知道下面有什么。

00:09:35.460 --> 00:09:37.460
We're going to take a leap of faith and see what happens.
我们将坚定信念，看看会发生什么。

00:09:37.460 --> 00:09:45.519
And it is really strange to me how little of a roadmap, we seem to have to answer that question.
对我来说真的很奇怪，我们似乎必须回答这个问题，路线图是如此之少。

00:09:45.519 --> 00:09:47.649
It's fascinating.
这很有趣。

00:09:47.649 --> 00:10:07.740
You know, I keep hearing this from parents of kids with disabilities over and over and over, that there's sort of a map, or there is no map that it feels like everyone's trying to tracing a different path and that path is sort of terrifyingly individual and also feels very communal in this way that is like hard to explain to people who aren't part of the group.
你知道，我一遍又一遍地从残疾儿童的父母那里听到这样的话，有某种地图，或者没有地图，感觉每个人都在试图追踪一条不同的道路，这条道路有点可怕的个人化，而且以这种方式感觉非常公共，这很难向不属于该群体的人解释。

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:10.539
Yes, it's very hard to explain it.
是的，这很难解释。

00:10:10.539 --> 00:10:16.677
Yeah, and the idea that you wrote a book that like takes people into the experience is so beautiful to me.
是的，你写一本让人们体验这种体验的书的想法对我来说真是太美好了。

00:10:16.677 --> 00:10:30.747
But I wonder if I can take you kind of back because you are in this world now and it sounds like you inhabit it so fully and are able in a way that like I think is really rare because I keep hearing this from parents, to bring other people in.
但我想知道我是否可以带你回去，因为你现在就在这个世界上，听起来你完全融入了这个世界，并且能够以一种我认为非常罕见的方式，因为我不断从父母那里听到这样的说法，把其他人带进来。

00:10:30.747 --> 00:10:38.563
But before you had Elliot, before you were in this group, before you were on this map, what was your relationship with disability-like?
但在你拥有艾略特之前，在你加入这个群体之前，在你出现在这张地图上之前，你与残疾的关系是什么？

00:10:38.563 --> 00:10:41.980
Like, you started working in TV in your 20s, is that right?
比如，你20多岁就开始在电视行业工作，是吗？

00:10:41.980 --> 00:10:43.179
Yeah, that's right.
是的，没错。

00:10:43.179 --> 00:10:46.700
What did your relationship to the disability community look like then?
当时您与残疾人社区的关系是什么样的？

00:10:47.419 --> 00:10:47.919
Yeah.
是的。

00:10:47.919 --> 00:10:49.712
I viewed it with...
我用...查看了它

00:10:49.712 --> 00:10:51.882
I didn't understand it.
我不明白。

00:10:51.882 --> 00:10:53.863
You know what I mean?
你知道我的意思？

00:10:53.863 --> 00:10:54.995
I viewed it.
我看过了。

00:10:54.995 --> 00:10:57.542
I had so much much naivete.
我有太多的天真。

00:10:57.542 --> 00:10:59.806
I had so much ignorance.
我真是太无知了。

00:10:59.806 --> 00:11:05.938
I always felt sort of a kinship with people who had disabilities.
我总是对残疾人有一种亲切感。

00:11:05.909 --> 00:11:13.963
I was friends with people who had disabilities in school, but I didn't have some larger understanding of what it really was.
我在学校里和一些残疾人是朋友，但我对残疾人的真正含义并没有更深入的了解。

00:11:13.963 --> 00:11:19.029
One of my closest friends had cerebral palsy and he was just this awesome guy.
我最亲密的朋友之一患有脑瘫，他是一个很棒的人。

00:11:19.029 --> 00:11:20.182
He was very athletic.
他非常有运动天赋。

00:11:20.182 --> 00:11:20.730
He limped.
他一瘸一拐的。

00:11:20.730 --> 00:11:25.778
He had different body challenges, but he was so good at baseball and tennis, he was amazing.
他面临着不同的身体挑战，但他非常擅长棒球和网球，他太棒了。

00:11:25.778 --> 00:11:26.985
He was just my friend.
他只是我的朋友。

00:11:26.985 --> 00:11:32.910
But I noticed as we got into junior high school in high school that people would make fun of him, sometimes.
但我注意到，当我们进入初中或高中时，人们有时会取笑他。

00:11:32.910 --> 00:11:37.134
And I thought to myself, this guy's a better athlete than you are, you jerks.
我心想，这家伙的运动能力比你们好，你们这些混蛋。

00:11:37.134 --> 00:11:39.109
What are you talking about, Stavoir?
你在说什么，斯塔瓦尔？

00:11:40.309 --> 00:11:42.605
And overcoming some certain challenges.
并克服一些特定的挑战。

00:11:42.605 --> 00:11:50.199
But it was funny, with that friend, who I was friends with all the way through high school, we never talked about his disability.
但有趣的是，和那个朋友，我在高中一直都是朋友，我们从来没有谈论过他的残疾。

00:11:50.199 --> 00:11:52.082
It was the 1980s into the 1990s.
那是 20 世纪 80 年代到 90 年代。

00:11:52.082 --> 00:12:32.110
We never really talked about it, or his identity, or what it meant, what it felt like he was just my friend, and I grew up at two, I have a cousin with who has Down Syndrome, and he, you know, we weren't close, we didn't live in the same town, but I always loved him, and he always had a great spirit, he's still with us, and he, I don't know, when you have somebody in your family with a disability, I think it does make you less inclined to be a bully or to make fun of people with disabilities, but it's not like it gives you, especially in the 80s and 90s, when I was growing up, it's not like there was some really robust, nuanced understanding of it or discussions about it.
我们从来没有真正谈论过它，或者他的身份，或者它意味着什么，感觉他只是我的朋友，我两岁时长大，我有一个患有唐氏综合症的表弟，他，你知道，我们并不亲密，我们不住在同一个城镇，但我一直爱他，他总是有一个伟大的精神，他仍然和我们在一起，而他，我不知道，当你家里有残疾人时，我认为这确实会让你不那么倾向于欺负残疾人或取笑残疾人，但这并没有给你带来什么，特别是在 80 年代和 90 年代，在我成长的过程中，并没有对它有一些真正强有力的、细致入微的理解或讨论。

00:12:32.110 --> 00:12:33.110
No, not at all.
不，一点也不。

00:12:33.110 --> 00:12:39.990
So I wouldn't say that I had a particularly deep understanding of it beyond the people I happened to know had disabilities.
所以我不会说我对它的理解比我碰巧认识的残疾人有特别深刻的理解。

00:12:39.990 --> 00:12:46.230
And I'm curious about what that looked like when you started in media, because I think we're in a similar age group.
我很好奇当你开始从事媒体工作时会是什么样子，因为我认为我们处于相似的年龄段。

00:12:46.230 --> 00:12:49.029
And what I remember is that- You're also a 35 grade.
我记得的是——你也是35级的。

00:12:49.029 --> 00:12:52.190
Yeah, I'm going to be- I've been 35 for like, maybe five years.
是的，我会——我已经 35 岁了，也许五年了。

00:12:52.190 --> 00:12:57.190
It's funny how you get there, and you just don't have an 18-year-old, I'm 35 anyway, it's just weird, yeah.
很有趣的是，你是如何到达那里的，而且你没有一个 18 岁的人，反正我已经 35 岁了，这很奇怪，是的。

00:12:57.190 --> 00:12:59.190
I'm so glad that we're both 35, it's a great age to be.
我很高兴我们都35岁了，这是一个伟大的年龄。

00:12:59.190 --> 00:13:00.190
We look amazing.
我们看起来棒极了。

00:13:00.190 --> 00:13:01.190
We do.
我们做到了。

00:13:01.190 --> 00:13:05.190
Of course, no work done.
当然，没有完成任何工作。

00:13:05.190 --> 00:13:15.190
But in that way that when you become conscious of something, I have learning disabilities, but they're invisible, no one who would speak to me would necessarily know.
但这样一来，当你意识到某件事时，我就有了学习障碍，但它们是无形的，没有人会和我说话一定会知道。

00:13:15.190 --> 00:13:17.190
I was always able to mask pretty effectively.
我总是能够非常有效地掩盖。

00:13:17.190 --> 00:13:21.190
I'm a girl with ADHD, so I was not just masking but fully missed.
我是一个患有多动症的女孩，所以我不仅仅是掩饰，而是完全错过了。

00:13:21.190 --> 00:13:21.690
missed.
错过了。

00:13:21.690 --> 00:13:23.482
And in some ways that served me.
在某些方面这对我很有帮助。

00:13:23.482 --> 00:13:26.786
But if I couldn't do something, I have to have dyscalculia.
但如果我不能做某事，我一定患有计算障碍。

00:13:26.786 --> 00:13:28.634
I can't do math and other things.
我不会做数学和其他事情。

00:13:28.634 --> 00:13:34.177
And if I was having a hard time doing something in my life, people have used the R word towards me.
如果我在生活中遇到困难，人们就会用“R”一词来形容我。

00:13:34.177 --> 00:13:35.018
What do you do?
你做什么工作？

00:13:35.018 --> 00:13:35.858
This, you know?
这个，你知道吗？

00:13:35.750 --> 00:13:36.250
Right.
正确的。

00:13:36.250 --> 00:13:36.750
Right.
正确的。

00:13:36.750 --> 00:13:37.250
Boy.
男生。

00:13:37.250 --> 00:13:38.504
Makes my blood boil.
让我热血沸腾。

00:13:38.504 --> 00:13:40.889
It's just makes my blood boil so much.
这让我热血沸腾。

00:13:40.889 --> 00:13:42.019
Please keep going.
请继续。

00:13:42.019 --> 00:13:44.654
I just need my blood to boil for a second.
我只需要我的血液沸腾一秒钟。

00:13:44.654 --> 00:13:46.599
No, a lot of the blood boiling.
不，很多人热血沸腾。

00:13:46.599 --> 00:13:50.302
That's what we we bring you here for is really a great you.
这就是我们带你来这里的原因，因为你真的很棒。

00:13:50.302 --> 00:13:58.020
But you know, I feel like at the time, it didn't, it was like upsetting, but I also like, it was such a commonly used word.
但你知道，我当时觉得，它没有，这就像令人不安，但我也喜欢，这是一个如此常用的词。

00:13:58.020 --> 00:14:11.386
And in my memory, it was pretty commonly used on television and in movies and in film and the way people with disabilities were depicted wasn't always, I mean, and I say was, was, was basically never thought over.
在我的记忆中，它在电视、电影和电影中非常普遍，而描绘残疾人的方式并不总是，我的意思是，我说是，是，基本上从未被考虑过。

00:14:10.889 --> 00:14:11.889
Never.
绝不。

00:14:11.889 --> 00:14:12.889
Yeah.
是的。

00:14:12.889 --> 00:14:13.889
It was portable and never.
它是便携式的，而且从来没有。

00:14:13.889 --> 00:14:14.889
Yes.
是的。

00:14:14.889 --> 00:14:18.293
started working in TV and film, like do you remember hearing it?
开始从事电视和电影工作，你还记得听说过吗？

00:14:18.293 --> 00:14:24.409
And I mean, I think I have like a picture of a writer's room in my mind and it's yeah, maybe not the kindest place.
我的意思是，我想我脑子里有一幅作家房间的照片，是的，也许不是最友善的地方。

00:14:26.090 --> 00:14:28.830
There are those writers rooms for sure.
肯定有那些作家室。

00:14:28.830 --> 00:14:33.537
There, yeah, still in reality, I'm going to be 50 in August, right?
是的，实际上，八月份我就满 50 岁了，对吧？

00:14:33.537 --> 00:14:39.228
When my writing partner, Carter and I, we got hired at age 22 on David Letterman.
当我的写作伙伴卡特和我 22 岁时，我们被《大卫·莱特曼》录用了。

00:14:39.228 --> 00:14:42.179
So we're these kids in the mid late 1990s.
我们都是 20 世纪 90 年代中后期的孩子。

00:14:42.179 --> 00:14:48.221
And 100% people threw around that word at that point, without thinking twice about it.
100%的人在那时就抛弃了这个词，没有多想。

00:14:48.221 --> 00:14:53.490
I even, I'll say this now because I've made such a crusade about this word.
我什至，我现在会这么说，因为我已经对这个词进行了如此大的改革。

00:14:53.490 --> 00:14:56.230
I wrote this essay in the Boston Globe.
我在《波士顿环球报》上写了这篇文章。

00:14:56.230 --> 00:15:03.958
I have published a short story and I were a review and now I've written a novel, all of which touch upon this.
我发表了一篇短篇小说，写了一篇评论，现在我写了一篇小说，所有这些都涉及到这一点。

00:15:03.958 --> 00:15:37.823
The larger story and feeling and experience of being the parent of some of this ability, but specifically within that the words that are used and the diminishing dehumanizing ways that people with disabilities continue to be talked about even in 2025 and sadly there seems to be a real rise in the culture of talking about it that way and I know we'll get into that right in a second but like to just turn it back on myself for a second so I don't sound preachy and holier than now.
作为某些这种能力的父母的更大的故事、感受和经历，但具体来说，即使在 2025 年，人们仍然继续谈论残疾人所使用的词语和逐渐减少的非人性化方式，可悲的是，以这种方式谈论它的文化似乎真正兴起，我知道我们马上就会讨论这个问题，但我想把它转回到我自己身上，这样我听起来就不会比现在更说教和神圣了。

00:15:37.823 --> 00:15:42.319
I didn't scold anyone for making are word jokes in 1997 or 2000.
在 1997 年或 2000 年，我没有责骂任何人开玩笑。

00:15:42.319 --> 00:15:44.076
I don't think anyone did.
我想没有人这么做过。

00:15:44.076 --> 00:15:48.572
To my great shame, I looked back not too long ago to screenplay.
令我非常羞愧的是，不久前我回顾了剧本。

00:15:48.572 --> 00:15:54.404
Unproduced and didn't go anywhere that Carter and I wrote that had that word in it.
未制作，也没有出现在卡特和我写的任何有这个词的地方。

00:15:54.404 --> 00:15:57.635
There was a joke where someone used that word.
有一个笑话，有人用了这个词。

00:15:57.635 --> 00:16:06.909
This is something that 25-year-old Craig thought it would be okay to put in that screenplay, not understanding anything at the time.
25 岁的克雷格认为将这些内容放入剧本中是可以的，但当时他什么都不懂。

00:16:06.909 --> 00:16:13.909
However old I was when I was working on that, almost 50-year-old Craig looked at this, recently like a couple months ago.
无论我从事这项工作时年龄多大，近 50 岁的克雷格最近（比如几个月前）都看过这个。

00:16:13.909 --> 00:16:19.190
I was looking back through this old screenplay and I found that word and I sat with it.
我在回顾这个旧剧本时发现了这个词，然后我就坐下来。

00:16:19.190 --> 00:16:21.190
It was like a punch in the face.
就像脸上挨了一拳。

00:16:21.190 --> 00:16:31.269
It was proof that I had used that word in the past and that I had committed it to paper and thought it was okay because in the late 1990s it was a comedy word.
这证明我过去曾使用过这个词，并且我已将其写在纸上，并认为它没问题，因为在 20 世纪 90 年代末，它是一个喜剧词。

00:16:31.269 --> 00:16:33.230
It was an edgy comedy word.
这是一个尖锐的喜剧词。

00:16:33.230 --> 00:16:39.509
It's like a funny punchline to say, and I hate to say it, it seems like it's coming back.
这就像一个有趣的妙语，我讨厌说它，它似乎又回来了。

00:16:39.509 --> 00:16:47.230
There are people in the culture now, and in politics now, dedicated to bringing this word back.
现在文化界和政治界都有人致力于将这个词带回来。

00:16:47.230 --> 00:16:48.230
They're celebrating it.
他们正在庆祝。

00:16:48.230 --> 00:16:49.230
They're celebrating it.
他们正在庆祝。

00:16:49.230 --> 00:16:50.230
They're celebrating it.
他们正在庆祝。

00:16:50.230 --> 00:16:51.230
Helping champagne.
帮忙开香槟。

00:16:51.230 --> 00:16:53.909
We've reclaimed this word.
我们已经收回了这个词。

00:16:53.909 --> 00:17:07.910
Like something has been achieved, like some great thing has happened by going back and finding this slur and saying, hey, no, we get to use a word that disparages the disability community to call anyone stupid and worthless.
就像已经取得了一些成就，就像发生了一些伟大的事情一样，回过头来发现这种诽谤并说，嘿，不，我们可以使用一个贬低残疾人群体的词来称任何人都是愚蠢和无价值的。

00:17:07.910 --> 00:17:12.347
By association saying the disability community is stupid and worthless.
联想到残疾人群体是愚蠢且毫无价值的。

00:17:12.347 --> 00:17:18.597
And the disconnect of seeing that, that those go hand in hand, they say, no, it's just a funny word.
看到这些是齐头并进的，他们说，不，这只是一个有趣的词。

00:17:18.597 --> 00:17:19.722
It's actually not.
其实不是。

00:17:19.722 --> 00:17:21.285
Words do actually matter.
言语确实很重要。

00:17:21.285 --> 00:17:22.910
Yeah, they mean something.
是的，它们意味着一些东西。

00:17:22.910 --> 00:17:27.630
They mean something and they shape how we think about certain types of people, right?
它们具有某种意义，并且塑造了我们对某些类型的人的看法，对吗？

00:17:27.630 --> 00:17:42.850
The other thing is, if you talk to somebody in the disability community, I've heard wonderfully eloquent anti-R word speeches by people with Down syndrome, by people who say, I do not like how this word diminishes me.
另一件事是，如果你与残疾人群体中的某个人交谈，我听到唐氏综合症患者雄辩地发表了雄辩的反 R 词演讲，他们说，我不喜欢这个词如何贬低我。

00:17:42.850 --> 00:17:47.549
And yet, the people who defend this word seem to say, no one cares.
然而，捍卫这个词的人似乎说，没有人在乎。

00:17:47.549 --> 00:17:54.340
Everyone knows it's a word that's just cool and funny and comedic and no one thinks it's related to disability.
每个人都知道这个词很酷、很有趣、很喜剧，但没有人认为它与残疾有关。

00:17:54.340 --> 00:18:06.024
That's just yet another lie because people with disabilities absolutely will tell you, no, that is a slur against my identity, like any other racial or religious epithets, slur, bigoted word.
这又是一个谎言，因为残疾人绝对会告诉你，不，这是对我身份的诽谤，就像任何其他种族或宗教绰号、诽谤、偏执词一样。

00:18:06.024 --> 00:18:06.758
It is right.
这是对的。

00:18:06.758 --> 00:18:08.165
The exact same as that.
和那个一模一样。

00:18:08.165 --> 00:18:15.506
There is no difference between those things, and it's very hard to convince certain people of that fact for some reason.
这些事情之间没有区别，并且出于某种原因很难让某些人相信这一事实。

00:18:15.506 --> 00:18:17.097
I'll never understand why.
我永远不会明白为什么。

00:18:17.097 --> 00:18:21.930
But to bring him out of his own, there was a time where I thought as young man.
但要让他走出自己的困境，我曾一度作为年轻人思考过。

00:18:21.930 --> 00:18:25.478
Apparently, I didn't even remember thinking this was true.
显然，我什至不记得我认为这是真的。

00:18:25.478 --> 00:18:31.473
We're using this word particularly much, but there was staring me in the face in the screen plane.
我们特别频繁地使用这个词，但屏幕平面上有人盯着我的脸。

00:18:31.473 --> 00:18:42.607
I really almost burst into tears when I saw it, because my son is now 18, and I've spent 18 years really reprogramming my mind and soul did, and just seeing the world so differently.
当我看到它时，我真的差点哭了，因为我的儿子现在 18 岁了，而我花了 18 年的时间真正重新编程了我的思想和灵魂，只是以不同的方式看待世界。

00:18:42.607 --> 00:18:53.680
So my son was born in 2007, which is still quite a while ago, and I think a lot has been learned about how to talk about disabilities since then, and certainly since the late 1990s.
我的儿子出生于 2007 年，距今已经有很长一段时间了，我认为从那时起，尤其是从 20 世纪 90 年代末以来，我们已经学到了很多关于如何谈论残疾的知识。

